11.4  Random numbers: /dev/urandom and entropy

TL;DR
  - Keys, tokens and session IDs need numbers nobody can predict. The kernel
    provides them: getrandom(), /dev/urandom.
  - It seeds a cryptographic generator with unpredictable events (interrupt
    timings, hardware generators), then can produce endless output safely.
  - In code, use your language's secure source: Python secrets,
    crypto.randomBytes in Node, rand::rngs::OsRng in Rust.

      head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64      # 32 random bytes
      openssl rand -hex 16                  # a 128-bit token

On your machine
  Trusted   3 certificate authorities, in /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
  AES       aes-aesni, with the CPU's AES instructions · SHA-256: sha256-ni
  RNG       hardware generator tpm-rng-0 feeds the kernel
  TPM       version 2: can hold keys and seal disk keys

Pitfalls
  - Math.random(), Python's random and C's rand() are predictable: never for
    passwords, tokens or keys.
  - Advice to use /dev/random "for more security" is outdated: since Linux 5.18
    both give the same output once the generator is seeded.
  - Cloning a VM from a snapshot used to duplicate its random state; current
    kernels reseed when the hypervisor reports a clone.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 11.4 --why

Related
  Commands  head, openssl
  Paths     /dev/urandom, /proc/sys/kernel/random/
  See also  idk 11.1 Hashes, encryption and signatures
  See also  idk 1.4 Interrupts: how devices get attention
